    "media:imagetext": "2 -\nThe welconing ceremony included the preparation and drinking of Yangona,\nthe native non-intoxteating drink.\nQuite a show. Then a spear dance by\nsinging of 50 years ago.\nHe spent the rest of the afternoon on the\nbeautiful beach in front of the Captaine \"bure\". Debby and I found sone\nfairly good shells. The beach and harbor are surrounded by mountains\nheavily wooded above a fringe of coconut palms which skirt the shore. The\nmeat of the coconut, or \"Copra\"\n› 1s the only cash crop of the Yasawas. The\ncoconuts are gathered by the natives and carried by boat to a central drying\nand shipping point.\nTuesday, Dec. 3» We salled early, past a very high mountain with\none side that drops away straight down for perhapa three or four hundred\nfeet. It is called \"Virgins leap\" because legend has it that some two :\nhundrod years ago some, invaders over-powered and killed all the warriors on\nthe island.\nThe wives and daughters having taken refuge on the mountain\ntop leaped oft the cliff. The natives have pronised to bury Capt. Withers\non that mountain top. We cruised among the islands with a rod out most of\nthe time. We took turns fishing and I was lucky enough to catch a 35 1b.\nyellow fin tuna, quite a battle. We stopped at a number of villages and\nbeaches including the \"Blue Lagoon\" a beautiful coral reef,- surrounded\nlagoon with thousands of small highly colored fish swiming in and out of\nthe multi-colored coral in the crystal clear water.\nOur final stop on our second day out was at Nacula island where\nwe anchored at 6 p.m. At this place as the others we went ashore in a\nsmall dinghy usually in two bout loads. After dinner on board the Captain\nannounced that the people of the island about thirty in all (excluding\nchildren) had Invited us to cone ashore for Tangona and a \"Hele\" or\ncelebration. They had bullt a big fire on the beach and in the light of\nthat fire they passed the Yangona - tastes like soapy water - when the\nwarrior in full war paint holds the coconut bowl straight out in front of\nhim you are supposed to clap your hands once, then hold out your boul into\nwhich he poures the Yangona.\nYou then must drink it all without stopping.\nWhen your cup is dry you toss 1t on the ground and the natives chant in\nFijian \"It is dry\". You then clap your hands three times and the ceremony,\nso far as you are concerned, is over.\nserved, sitting on the ground, the natives started singing to the accomplament\nof two or three ukes and one guitar.\nthe first place their language, as Hawaiian, is composed of all vowels and\nthere is no harsh sound, then their voices and the music are both good and\nfinally they sing the parts to perfection. Of course they just Love any\nexcuse to have a celebration. In the early daye the Missionarys taught\nthem that dancing was very wicked. Of course hymn singing was very good.\nThe natives loved the hymns, that was ok - but they still wanted to dance\n3o the missionarys compromised and let them dance the \"Taralala\" wherein\nyou stand side by side with your partner putting your arn around her and\nshe puts her arm around you. Then you start to shuffle to the musio and\nbreak away when it stops.\nThe moon came out casting long shadows of the\ntall coconut palms on the white beach with the water gently lapping the\nshore.\nJE1 PI00",
